#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:1
msgid "Software Pipeline Annotations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:3
msgid ""
"TileLang can infer common producer/consumer pipelines from `T.Pipelined(..., "
"num_stages=...)`. For regular GEMM-like kernels, this is the preferred entry "
"point:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:7
msgid ""
"for ko in T.Pipelined(T.ceildiv(K, BK), num_stages=3):\n"
"    T.copy(A[by * BM, ko * BK], A_shared)\n"
"    T.copy(B[ko * BK, bx * BN], B_shared)\n"
"    T.gemm(A_shared, B_shared, C_local)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:14
msgid ""
"For kernels whose loop body has unusual ordering, extra post-processing, or "
"manual async-copy grouping, you can provide explicit pipeline annotations. "
"This guide explains how to write those annotations and why replayable scalar "
"`Bind` statements are not part of the user-visible schedule."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:19
msgid "The User Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:21
msgid ""
"Pipeline annotations describe executable pipeline statements, not every IR "
"node inside the loop body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:24
msgid "Annotate the statements that do work:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:26
msgid "copies, including global-to-shared and TMA copies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:27
msgid "fills"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:28
msgid "GEMM or other tile operations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:29
msgid "reductions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:30
msgid "stores and atomics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:31
msgid ""
"explicit waits, commits, or synchronization statements when they are part of "
"the manual pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:34
msgid "Do not annotate replayable scalar aliases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:36
msgid ""
"base: T.int32 = ko * BK\n"
"offset: T.int32 = base + tx\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:41
msgid ""
"These `Bind` statements are value definitions, not materialized storage. The "
"compiler places them automatically at each use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:44
msgid "Stage and Order"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:46
msgid "Each scheduled statement has two numbers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:48
msgid "`stage`: the logical pipeline stage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:49
msgid "`order`: the order used when emitting scheduled statements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:51
msgid ""
"Lower stages run earlier in the pipeline. A typical copy/compute pipeline "
"has copy statements in stage 0 and compute statements in stage 1:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:54
msgid ""
"for ko in T.Pipelined(\n"
"    T.ceildiv(K, BK),\n"
"    stage=[0, 0, 1],\n"
"    order=[0, 1, 2],\n"
"):\n"
"    T.copy(A[ko * BK], A_shared)\n"
"    T.copy(B[ko * BK], B_shared)\n"
"    T.gemm(A_shared, B_shared, C_local)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:65
msgid ""
"The two arrays are aligned to the scheduled statements in source order. In "
"the example above:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:68
msgid ""
"statement 0: copy A  -> stage 0, order 0\n"
"statement 1: copy B  -> stage 0, order 1\n"
"statement 2: gemm    -> stage 1, order 2\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:74
msgid ""
"The compiler checks buffer dependencies after applying the annotations. If "
"one scheduled statement produces data for another, the producer must not be "
"placed after the consumer. In practice:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:78
msgid ""
"If producer and consumer are in the same stage, producer order must be "
"smaller than consumer order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:80
msgid ""
"If they are in different stages, producer stage must be less than or equal "
"to consumer stage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:83
msgid ""
"When `stage` and `order` are provided manually, do not also set `num_stages` "
"in normal code. The pipeline depth is inferred from the stage list as "
"`max(stage) + 1`. Use `num_stages` by itself for compiler-inferred "
"pipelines, and use `stage` / `order` by themselves for manually scheduled "
"pipelines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:88
msgid "Annotating a Reordered Pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:90
msgid ""
"The `order` array is useful when the pipeline should issue a future-"
"iteration producer before a current-iteration consumer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:93
msgid ""
"for ko in T.Pipelined(\n"
"    num_tiles,\n"
"    stage=[0, 1],\n"
"    order=[1, 0],\n"
"):\n"
"    T.copy(A[ko * BK], A_shared)\n"
"    T.gemm(A_shared, B_shared, C_local)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:103
msgid "This says there are two scheduled statements:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:105
msgid ""
"copy -> stage 0, order 1\n"
"gemm -> stage 1, order 0\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:110
msgid ""
"During pipeline rewriting, the compiler emits prologue, steady-state body, "
"and epilogue pieces with the proper logical iteration for each stage. The "
"source order still determines which annotation entry belongs to which "
"scheduled statement; `order` controls the emission order after the "
"statements have been classified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:116
msgid "Replayable Scalar Bind Statements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:118
msgid "A replayable scalar `Bind` may appear as a statement in the loop body:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:120
msgid ""
"for ko in T.Pipelined(\n"
"    num_tiles,\n"
"    stage=[0, 1],\n"
"    order=[1, 0],\n"
"):\n"
"    base: T.int32 = ko * BK\n"
"    T.copy(A[base + tx], A_shared[tx])\n"
"    T.gemm(A_shared, B_shared, C_local)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:131
msgid ""
"The `stage` and `order` arrays contain two entries, not three. They annotate "
"the copy and the GEMM. The `base` definition is intentionally omitted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:134
msgid ""
"This rule matters because a replayable `Bind` does not have a unique "
"pipeline position. It is closer to an SSA value or a local alias than to an "
"executable operation. If the same scalar is used by statements in different "
"stages, each consumer may need the value under a different logical pipeline "
"iteration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:139
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:141
msgid ""
"for ko in T.Pipelined(\n"
"    num_tiles,\n"
"    stage=[0, 1],\n"
"    order=[1, 0],\n"
"):\n"
"    base: T.int32 = ko * BK\n"
"    T.copy(A[base + tx], A_shared[tx])\n"
"    B[base + tx] = A_shared[tx]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:152
msgid ""
"After pipeline rewriting, the copy may need `base` for a future producer "
"iteration while the store needs `base` for the current consumer iteration. A "
"single user-selected stage/order for `base` would either be out of scope or "
"would describe the wrong logical iteration for one of the uses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:157
msgid "TileLang therefore treats replayable scalar `Bind` as non-schedulable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:159
msgid ""
"stage/order annotate only scheduled, effectful statements\n"
"replayable Bind definitions are replayed at each consumer\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:164
msgid ""
"The replay is use-driven. If a consumer statement references a scalar bound "
"in the pipeline body, the compiler recreates the needed `Bind` immediately "
"before that consumer and substitutes the consumer's logical access index."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:168
msgid ""
"A replayable scalar `Bind` may contain a read from a buffer that is not "
"written inside the pipeline body:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:171
msgid ""
"idx: T.int32 = Ids[ko]\n"
"T.copy(A[idx], A_shared)\n"
"B[idx] = A_shared[0]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:177
msgid ""
"Here `idx` is still a value alias. If both scheduled statements use it, "
"TileLang replays `idx = Ids[logical_ko]` for each consumer. This may "
"duplicate the load from `Ids`, but it preserves the alias semantics of "
"`Bind`: the value is computed at the consumer's logical pipeline iteration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:182
msgid "This is different from a materialized producer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:184
msgid ""
"idx_shared[tx] = Ids[ko]\n"
"T.copy(A[idx_shared[tx]], A_shared)\n"
"B[idx_shared[tx]] = A_shared[0]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:190
msgid ""
"The store to `idx_shared` creates storage and a real producer/consumer "
"dependency. It should be counted as a scheduled statement and, when needed, "
"versioned like other pipeline buffers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:194
msgid ""
"If a `Bind` reads a buffer that is written inside the same pipeline body, it "
"is not treated as replayable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:197
msgid ""
"T.copy(A[ko * BK], A_shared)\n"
"value: T.float32 = A_shared[tx]\n"
"C[ko * BK + tx] = value\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:203
msgid ""
"The load from `A_shared` depends on a pipeline producer. TileLang keeps this "
"`Bind` in the scheduled statement list, so it needs a `stage` and `order` "
"entry. Because a scalar `Bind` has no storage versioning, a scheduled `Bind` "
"must be in the same stage as every consumer that uses its value. If the "
"intended semantics are \"load once and share across later stages\", "
"materialize the value explicitly with a buffer/register write instead of "
"relying on `Bind` replay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:210
msgid "Bind Dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:212
msgid "Replayable binds can depend on earlier replayable binds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:214
msgid ""
"base: T.int32 = ko * BK\n"
"offset: T.int32 = base + tx\n"
"T.copy(A[offset], A_shared[tx])\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:220
msgid "The compiler replays dependencies before their users:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:222
msgid ""
"base\n"
"offset\n"
"consumer statement\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:228
msgid ""
"This keeps manual annotations focused on actual pipeline operations while "
"still preserving the lexical scalar dependencies from the original loop body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:231
msgid "Legacy Annotation Form"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:233
msgid ""
"Older code may include replayable scalar `Bind` statements in the annotation "
"arrays:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:236
msgid ""
"for ko in T.Pipelined(\n"
"    num_tiles,\n"
"    stage=[3, 0, 1],\n"
"    order=[1, 2, 0],\n"
"):\n"
"    base: T.int32 = ko * BK\n"
"    T.copy(A[base + tx], A_shared[tx])\n"
"    B[base + tx] = A_shared[tx]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:247
msgid ""
"This form is accepted for compatibility. The scalar `Bind` entry is ignored, "
"and the remaining entries are applied to the scheduled statements:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:250
msgid ""
"base Bind -> ignored\n"
"copy      -> stage 0, order 2\n"
"store     -> stage 1, order 0\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:256
msgid ""
"When a legacy replayable scalar `Bind` annotation is used by multiple "
"scheduled statements, TileLang may warn that the scalar annotation is "
"ignored and the bind is replayed at each use. New code should prefer the "
"shorter annotation arrays that omit replayable scalar binds entirely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:261
msgid "Manual `T.serial` Annotations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:263
msgid ""
"Most user code should use `T.Pipelined`. Lower-level tests or transform-"
"level code may use the raw loop annotations directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:266
msgid ""
"for ko in T.serial(\n"
"    0,\n"
"    num_tiles,\n"
"    annotations={\n"
"        \"software_pipeline_stage\": [0, 1],\n"
"        \"software_pipeline_order\": [1, 0],\n"
"        \"software_pipeline_async_stages\": [0],\n"
"    },\n"
"):\n"
"    base: T.int32 = ko * BK\n"
"    T.copy(A[base + tx], A_shared[tx])\n"
"    B[base + tx] = A_shared[tx]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:281
msgid ""
"The same rule applies: `software_pipeline_stage` and "
"`software_pipeline_order` describe only scheduled statements. Replayable "
"scalar `Bind` statements do not need entries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:285
msgid "Design Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:287
msgid "The pipeline pass separates the loop body into two concepts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:289
msgid ""
"scheduled statements: executable operations controlled by stage/order\n"
"replayable scalar bindings: local value aliases placed by use-def analysis\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:294
msgid ""
"This split keeps the API stable for users and avoids ambiguous schedules. A "
"real pipeline operation has a clear execution point, can read or write "
"buffers, and may require async-copy bookkeeping or synchronization. It is "
"appropriate for the user to assign a stage and order to that operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:299
msgid "A replayable scalar `Bind` has none of those properties:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:301
msgid "It has no side effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:302
msgid "It owns no buffer storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:303
msgid "It can be used by multiple scheduled statements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:304
msgid ""
"Its correct value may depend on the consumer's logical pipeline iteration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:306
msgid ""
"Forcing users to annotate such a statement would require them to choose one "
"stage/order even when there is no single correct answer. Replaying scalar "
"binds at each consumer makes the alias semantics explicit in the generated "
"IR and keeps the user-facing schedule focused on real pipeline work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:311
msgid "Checklist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:313
msgid "When writing manual pipeline annotations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:315
msgid "Count only scheduled statements when building `stage` and `order`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:316
msgid ""
"Omit replayable scalar aliases such as `base = ko * BK` or `idx = Ids[ko]` "
"when `Ids` is not written in the pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:318
msgid ""
"Count a `Bind` as a scheduled statement if it reads a buffer written inside "
"the pipeline body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:320
msgid "Keep each scheduled statement's `order` unique."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:321
msgid ""
"Keep producers before consumers according to stage/order dependency rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:322
msgid ""
"Use `software_pipeline_async_producers` and "
"`software_pipeline_async_producer_groups` with the same length as the "
"scheduled statement list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../programming_guides/software_pipeline.md:325
msgid ""
"Prefer the bind-free form for replayable aliases in new code; rely on legacy "
"bind slots only when maintaining old kernels."
msgstr ""
